WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.200
- The Negro Leagues were a benefactor of

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.250
World War II in a secondary way.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.430
The war broadened the mindset

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.390
and opened up an opportunity for

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.630
white Americans to see that

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:13.487
perhaps we need to make

00:00:13.487 --> 00:00:15.550
the field level here.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:18.230
- The major leagues were
supposed to be the best.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:19.840
But then the major league teams

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.670
would get beaten by a team

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.330
that had all Black people on it,

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.530
people that are supposed to be inferior.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:29.563
- They knew they had
to break that barrier.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.340
- Newspapers and newspaper writers,

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:34.173
very important,

00:00:34.173 --> 00:00:37.420
because they were always
telling the story.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.570
Especially Black publications.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.150
- They were responsible for

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:43.010
documenting this history

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.290
that you didn't read about,

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:45.810
hear about in national news.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.360
It was really in our Black newspaper.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.913
And one of those I wanna
salute is Sam Lacy.

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:55.160
- Sam Lacy is, outside of my father,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.870
the biggest influence in my life.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.750
- I think one of the most important

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:00.350
journalists in the annals of

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.170
American journalistic history.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:03.860
- He's in the National
Baseball Hall of Fame's

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:04.830
writers' wing.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.081
He was at every major
sporting event in America.

00:01:08.081 --> 00:01:10.714
- He became the voice of Black baseball.

00:01:10.714 --> 00:01:12.870
- Sam had a unique writing style.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.110
- You couldn't look past the truth

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.240
that was in Sam Lacy's stories.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.607
- One of his quotes was,

00:01:18.607 --> 00:01:20.990
"All men are created equal,

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.940
but some men are created
more equal than others."

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.640
And that was his definition
of white privilege.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.476
- Sam Lacy was very
instrumental in getting

00:01:27.476 --> 00:01:31.400
Major League Baseball to integrate.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.830
He had several conversations

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:34.430
in meeting with Clark Griffith,

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.590
the owner of the Washington Senators.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.650
According to Sam, many of those meetings

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.030
did not go very well.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:41.830
There were a lot of promises made.

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:43.320
None were ever kept.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.610
- Clark Griffith made so much money

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:46.770
from renting his stadium

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:48.360
to the Homestead Grays

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.950
that a lot of these owners

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.640
were against integration

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.160
because they knew if
Black players were playing

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.110
for white Major League Baseball teams

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.550
and there were no more Negro league games,

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.140
then they would lose
that significant chunk

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.410
of revenue that they'd been earning.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.080
- But Sam was persistent,

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.640
and he wrote a lot of articles

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.190
challenging Clark Griffith to uphold

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:12.423
his commitment to integrate the game.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.290
- There was Kenesaw Mountain Landis,

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:16.210
who was the commissioner of

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:17.043
Major League Baseball,

00:02:17.043 --> 00:02:20.210
was very vocal in his stance

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.650
that as long as he was commissioner,

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.050
there was never going to be

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.750
a Black man playing in the major leagues.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.000
- In my opinion, one of the most

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.310
racist men in baseball history.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.179
- He's on record telling Rube Foster that,

00:02:32.179 --> 00:02:34.700
"You give our league a black eye

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.920
when your teams beat our teams!"

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.060
- He had an opportunity
to fix the game early,

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.600
but he refused to accept

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.320
the responsibility, and the game was

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.023
not repaired until he died in 1944.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.960
- Happy Chandler becomes
the next commissioner.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.110
And Happy Chandler opens the door

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:56.320
for Jackie Robinson to become the

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.880
first Black player to
play in the major leagues

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.990
in Major League Baseball's
organized history.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:03.630
- When we talk about Branch Rickey

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.210
signing Jackie Robinson

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.070
and integrating Major League Baseball,

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.100
he pushed for integration

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.490
in a way that didn't honor the

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.080
desires and the needs of
everyone else around him.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.633
- Kansas City Monarchs
received zero dollars.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:20.190
They got nothing for Jackie Robinson.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.860
- October of 1945, Jackie's
deal was announced.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.000
And spring of '46, Branch Rickey signed

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.210
other players in addition
to Jackie Robinson.

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.880
Branch Rickey is in their backyard.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:33.950
He is continuing to steal players,

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.330
without honoring the Negro leagues,

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.470
without honoring their contracts.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.110
And the Negro National League owners

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:41.060
were absolutely livid.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.690
He essentially put the Negro league owners

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:46.160
between a rock and a poorhouse.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:47.490
- There's no way

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:50.450
that the other owners
can stand up and protest

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.570
what every Black baseball fan

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:53.973
in America had been waiting,

00:03:53.973 --> 00:03:56.020
and that was for a Black man

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:57.470
to play in the major leagues.

00:03:59.355 --> 00:04:02.102
Effa Manley is the most important female

00:04:02.102 --> 00:04:03.840
in baseball history,

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.170
Black or white.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.020
She's the first woman to be inducted into

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.000
the National Baseball Hall of Fame.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.040
- She was the Queen of the Negro Leagues.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.230
- Effa married Abe Manley,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.820
and Abe was a huge baseball fan.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.900
So, in 1935 he purchased
the Newark Eagles.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.880
From the very beginning, he relied on her

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:24.360
to kind of run the
business side of things.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.430
As co-owner, Effa pretty
much did everything

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:29.670
except perhaps player scouting.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.370
But she handled player
contracts and negotiations.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.712
She scheduled games.

00:04:33.712 --> 00:04:36.322
She bought uniforms and equipment.

00:04:36.322 --> 00:04:38.080
Perhaps most importantly,

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:41.070
she was responsible for public relations

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:42.340
and community relations.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.230
She was constantly trying
to figure out a way

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:47.010
to use her platform with this team

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.430
to give back to this community.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:53.060
- As they integrated the
major leagues, Effa held out.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:55.160
- I think Effa knew just as well

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.130
as all the other owners knew

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.110
that baseball's integration was going

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:01.880
to kill the Negro leagues.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:02.713
- Branch Rickey,

00:05:02.713 --> 00:05:04.560
not too long after Jackie Robinson,

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.044
comes calling for Monte Irvin.

00:05:06.044 --> 00:05:09.170
- Monte Irvin was a superstar player.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:12.600
Well, Effa Manley essentially refused

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.700
to allow him to be signed by

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:15.800
the Brooklyn Dodgers

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.730
without it being contested.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:18.563
- She felt like,

00:05:18.563 --> 00:05:20.097
"I have contracts on these men.

00:05:20.097 --> 00:05:22.450
And if you want them, you will pay me."

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:24.290
She was criticized by the Black press

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.526
for hindering integration,

00:05:26.526 --> 00:05:28.380
but she stood her ground.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.340
- Effa always knew she was going to do

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:31.600
whatever she had to do to make sure

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:32.790
that he had an opportunity to play

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.130
Major League Baseball.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.120
So, she ended up negotiating

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.380
with the owners of

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:38.660
the New York Giants.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.534
They agreed to buy his
contract for $5,000.

00:05:41.534 --> 00:05:44.650
Again, far less than what
he was actually worth.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:46.500
- So, essentially Jackie's stolen,

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.010
but she got compensated for people like

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:50.180
Don Newcombe,

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:51.260
Larry Doby.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:54.040
- Her stance and fighting for compensation

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.750
for her players is what essentially opened

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:59.120
this up for other Negro league owners

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:00.929
to at least start getting paid

00:06:00.929 --> 00:06:03.021
for players who they would then sell

00:06:03.021 --> 00:06:04.610
to the major leagues.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:05.840
But I think all the owners

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:06.981
at that point in time realized

00:06:06.981 --> 00:06:09.380
that the handwriting was on the wall.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:10.680
It wasn't a matter of if

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:12.440
the Negro leagues were going to fold.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.993
It was simply a matter of
when they were going to fold.

